  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Soaking rain is expected areawide Wednesday \nnight.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION... \nA broad low pressure system in northern Canada will bring a \nstrong warm front through Maine Wednesday night, with the \nassociated cold front quickly exiting the region late Wednesday\nnight. Anticipating rainfall to move in from the west Wednesday\nnight, becoming area wide and moderate after midnight. Cannot \nfully rule out am embedded thunderstorm, but not anticipating \nsevere weather given very marginal instability. \n\nAs of this update, rainfall totals should reach between \n0.5 to 0.75 inches throughout the region, with the Downeast\nregion getting the most rain. Localized higher totals are \npossible with heavier convection that develops overnight. \nFlooding is not a concern with this system, but rain amounts \nshould give some relief to the drought. As the system exits on \nThursday, some wrap around moisture from the system could bring \nsome isolated showers through the north.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Strong gusty winds expected with the rain \nWednesday night and again during the day Thursday and Friday. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION... As mentioned above, strong low \npressure system passes to the north of the forecast area, with \nstrong winds aloft. This will bring strong gusts late Wednesday \nnight in the Downeast region, and again during the day Thursday \nfrom the west, and again from the west during the day Friday. \nThe winds aloft will be the strongest Wednesday night, with up \nto about 55-60 kt at 850mb, but there will be lower surface \ngusts due to stable surface layer. More realistically, we are \nlooking at gusts as high as about 30 mph from the south \nWednesday night, with gusts potentially up to 35 mph. Low level \njet develops Wednesday night, bringing strong gusts over the \nwaters. Gusts around 35 mph from the west appear likely \nThursday, and with wet soils after Wednesday night's rain, plus \nleaves on the trees, there could be very isolated tree \ndamage/power outages. Wind gusts gradually relax Thursday \nevening, but pick back up on Friday with gusts up to 35 mph\nagain possible, mainly over the north.",
